    "explanationEn": "🎯 Why (B - 'Stating informal real-world knowledge in formal logic terms is extremely difficult, and deductive reasoning can be computationally intractable') is the correct choice:\nThe logicist approach struggles because formalizing informal world knowledge is extremely hard, and pure deductive reasoning can be computationally intractable.\n\n💡 Real-World Example & Application:\nModern intelligent systems like diagnostic medical engines and conversational agents rely on these historical foundations for rational decision-making.\n\n❌ Why other options are incorrect:\n• (A - Computers cannot perform logical operations like AND and OR): Digital computers are physically engineered from electronic logic gates executing boolean AND/OR flawlessly.\n• (C - Aristotelian syllogisms only function in continuous environments): Aristotelian syllogisms operate on discrete propositions and categories, not continuous topologies.\n• (D - Formal logic cannot be implemented using programming languages): Formal logic is readily implemented in programming paradigms like logic programming (Prolog) and theorem provers.",
